          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "**Does LDL alone decide treatment?** No. The lipid guidance says the decision is driven by estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, not by the LDL number alone (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*).\n\n**What risk bands does the guidance use?** The passages I retrieved don't give the lipid risk bands or cut-off numbers. The lipid document's risk section was cut off before those details, so I can't tell you what band you would fall into. The only band I found is in the hypertension guidance, which uses an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more as one trigger for starting blood-pressure drug treatment. That applies to blood pressure decisions, not to LDL. A clinician can calculate your 10-year estimate and explain how it applies to you.\n\n**Which modifiable risk factors does the corpus describe?** The general reference on coronary artery disease lists these contributors:\n- Smoking\n- High blood pressure\n- Raised LDL cholesterol\n- Diabetes\n- Obesity\n- Physical inactivity\n- A diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber\n- Chronic kidney disease\n- Inflammatory conditions\n\nIt says age and male sex cannot be changed, and the rest of the list can be. It also lists family history of early coronary disease as a contributor, though it isn't clear whether that counts as modifiable. The same document says managing these factors makes the risk estimate useful in practice.\n\n**What the results didn't cover:** Whether your LDL level is high for you, the exact risk bands, and any treatment steps for your situation. Those need a clinician who knows your full history, including blood pressure, blood sugar, smoking status and family history.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
